// Package fp holds the float64 helpers `pkg/std/math` leaves to the caller:
// naming a value's IEEE class, and converting to an integer without silently
// losing the fact that it did not fit.
//
// Both exist because of a rule that surprises people coming from C: in Binate
// EVERY arithmetic operation is defined, so nothing here can trap or be
// undefined. A float→int `cast` SATURATES at the integer extremes (and NaN
// becomes 0) rather than producing garbage, and comparisons follow IEEE, so
// `x != x` is the NaN test and `-0.0 == 0.0` is true even though the two have
// different bit patterns. Defined does not mean lossless — these two functions
// are how a caller notices.
package "pkg/fp"

// Class names the IEEE class of x: "nan", "+inf", "-inf", "zero", "-zero",
// "subnormal", or "normal".
//
// Zero is split because the sign of zero survives arithmetic and is observable
// (`math.Signbit`, `1/x`) even though `-0.0 == 0.0` compares true — so a
// classifier that reported only "zero" would hide a real distinction.
func Class(x float64) @[]readonly char

// ToInt64 converts x the way `cast(int64, x)` does — truncating toward zero —
// and additionally reports whether the value SURVIVED: false when x was NaN
// (which becomes 0) or outside the int64 range (which saturates at the extreme).
// The conversion itself never traps; the bool is the only way to tell a genuine
// 9223372036854775807 from a saturated one.
//
// int64 rather than the word-sized int, so the bounds below are the same on a
// 32-bit target as on a 64-bit one.
func ToInt64(x float64) (int64, bool)

// Ulp returns the distance from x to the next representable float64 away from
// zero — the size of one unit in the last place. It is the scale at which
// float comparisons stop being meaningful: near 1.0 it is about 2.2e-16, near
// 1e16 it is 2.0, and beyond 2^53 it exceeds 1, which is why a large float64
// cannot represent consecutive integers.
func Ulp(x float64) float64
